-Visual symbolic systems embody ambiguity, polysemy, and context-dependent meaning. Applied to such systems, language models typically reduce each symbol to one representation and settle on a single reading, leaving out how context reweights the relations among its meanings. The Lakota Shape Kit, a traditional storytelling tool, exemplifies this richness. Each symbol juxtaposes many concurrent readings whose prominence shifts with narrative context. Working from Lakota relational epistemology (\emph{Mitákuye Oyás'i\textipa{N}}, the principle that nothing exists in isolation and that meaning is a thing's position in a living web of relations), we develop a framework that represents each symbol as a context-weighted distribution over semantic descriptors and then asks not merely \emph{which} descriptors a context foregrounds, but \emph{how a context recouples the relations among them}. Our central instrument is the $\Delta$ coupling matrix, the signed change in the pairwise inner-product (Gram) structure of a curated lexicon under a context-shift operator. $\Delta$ localizes contextualization to the lexical relations that strengthen or weaken, providing a grounded, interpretable readout of how narrative framing rewires a symbolic field. The readout re-expresses how the encoder relates a context to each descriptor as a first-order, relevance-gated recoupling in the community-curated coordinate system, and we demonstrate it across several settings. We read a narrative as a trajectory through coupling space, use the recoupling margin to flag readings the field leaves unsettled, recover content-bearing context similarity once a single activation-magnitude artifact is removed, and, because the readout is modality-agnostic, probe the field's ambiguity with sound as well as text. Developed in collaboration with a knowledge holder under the Abundant Intelligences program, the framework advances context-sensitive meaning modeling for computational linguistics, cross-cultural knowledge representation, and creative AI.
+Visual symbolic systems embody ambiguity, polysemy, and context-dependent meaning. Creative practitioners increasingly meet such systems through general language models, which reduce each symbol to one representation and read it through associations other than those of the communities who hold it. The Lakota Shape Kit, a traditional storytelling tool, exemplifies this richness. Each symbol juxtaposes many concurrent readings whose prominence shifts with narrative context. Working from Lakota relational epistemology (\emph{Mitákuye Oyás'i\textipa{N}}, the principle that nothing exists in isolation and that meaning is a thing's position in a living web of relations), we ask how a community's own symbolic vocabulary can become the ground on which a model's associations are read, navigated, and questioned in creative practice. With an Oglala Lakota knowledge holder, we represent each symbol by curated descriptors and read a text or a sound through the relations among those descriptors that it strengthens, summarized in a $\Delta$ coupling matrix. We read a story as a path through the field, use a confidence margin to tell settled readings from open ones, compare contexts by how they engage the field, and drive the field with sound. The readout's symbol-level signal matches plain similarity to the descriptors, so $\Delta$ contributes a relational view rather than new information, and one symbol tends to absorb readings that the tradition would spread across several. Together with the creative practices the instrument opens and the community review on which its validation rests, these findings outline AI tools that answer to the traditions they read.
