+Modern language embeddings are, in fact, remarkably good at \emph{encoding} ambiguity. A trained model packs many senses of a word into a single vector, and contextualized encoders adjust that vector fluidly as the surrounding text changes \cite{haber_polysemyevidence_2024, loureiro_analysis_2021}. The difficulty is not that models cannot represent polysemy, but that this latent ambiguity is neither \emph{legible} nor \emph{relational}. The senses are entangled in an opaque coordinate system rather than organized by the categories of a particular symbolic tradition; meanings underrepresented in training data may be faint or absent (a limitation of data more than of architecture); and, most importantly for a relational epistemology, the encoder exposes the contextualized \emph{vector} of a symbol but not how a context reshapes the web of \emph{relations} among a curated field's facets, which is the level at which meaning here actually lives \cite{ferrari_identification_2018}. The gap, then, is a gap in \emph{instruments}. We lack ways to read how a model represents a community-curated symbolic system and how a context bears on it. For creative practitioners who now meet cultural symbols through such models, the gap has a practical cost, since the model's defaults silently stand in for the tradition's own categories.
+
+This paper addresses that gap and is guided by one question. How can a community's own symbolic vocabulary become the ground on which a language model's associations are read, navigated, and questioned in creative practice, so that a tool follows the tradition's categories rather than the model's defaults? Large language models already shift a symbol's meaning fluidly with context (this is, in effect, what their attention layers do), but that machinery is rarely made inspectable at the level of a set of symbolic anchors. We therefore treat a curated lexicon as a controlled probe of a language model's embedding space. Each symbol is represented as a distribution over textual descriptors, and we measure the signed change in the pairwise coupling among those descriptors when a context shifts them, turning how the model relates a context to the field into a legible, per-relation readout. We call this \textbf{quantifying how context recouples the latent semantics} of the field.
+
+Our contribution is threefold. (1) We cast a community-curated symbolic system as the coordinate system through which a language model is read, so that its associations become inspectable in the categories that matter to the community rather than in an opaque geometry. (2) We build an instrument around this idea, centred on the $\Delta$ coupling matrix as a relational view of which descriptor relations a text or a sound strengthens, and demonstrate it on narrative paths, confidence, context comparison, and sound. (3) We draw out the creative practices the instrument supports and report openly where the model's associations diverge from the tradition, treating these divergences as part of what the instrument shows and leaving validation against the living tradition to community review. Ultimately this research aims to inform AI methodologies developed in active collaboration with Indigenous research projects and grounded in their knowledge systems.
