-\subsection{The recoupling margin as an ambiguity meter}
-The trajectory view tracks \emph{where} the recoupling points at each step; we now ask \emph{how decisively} it points there. The same de-biased per-symbol response that drives the timeline in Figure~\ref{fig:narrative} has a top-1 and a top-2 at any given context, and their \emph{margin} measures how sharply the context resolves the field to a single symbol. A large margin marks a settled reading; a small margin marks a genuinely open one, where two or more symbols remain in play. The margin is thus an ambiguity meter, and Figure~\ref{fig:ambiguity} shows it behaving as one. On a battery of thirty indirect single-target probes, short phrases designed to evoke one symbol without naming it, the margin separates the readout's correct readings from its misses (AUC $\approx 0.78$, 20 correct and 10 missed, mean margin $0.36$ against $0.17$). A low margin therefore flags a reading the readout should not commit to. Six phrases deliberately constructed so that two symbols fit equally well fall in the same low-margin region (mean margin $0.19$). In such cases the response profile shows which symbols are in tension, and when the intended symbol is not among them, the low margin is the warning (Figure~\ref{fig:ambiguity}C2). This is the signal a system for navigating ambiguity needs. Where the margin is high, it can commit to one reading; where it is low, it can surface the competing interpretations rather than force a single answer.
