+\subsection{The recoupling margin as a confidence cue}
+The trajectory view tracks \emph{where} the recoupling points at each step; we now ask \emph{how decisively} it points there. The same de-biased per-symbol response that drives the timeline in Figure~\ref{fig:narrative} has a top-1 and a top-2 at any given context, and their \emph{margin} measures how sharply the context resolves the field to a single symbol. A large margin marks a settled reading, and a small margin a reading that leaves several symbols in play. Figure~\ref{fig:ambiguity} shows the margin behaving as a confidence cue. On a battery of thirty indirect single-target probes, short phrases designed to evoke one symbol without naming it, the margin separates the readings that match the intended symbol from those that do not (AUC $\approx 0.78$, 20 matching and 10 not, mean margin $0.36$ against $0.17$). The battery is also the one used to calibrate the readout, but calibrating each probe on the other twenty-nine leaves the readings unchanged (AUC $0.82$). Six phrases written so that two symbols fit equally well also fall in the low-margin region (mean margin $0.19$). A low margin therefore signals that the field has not settled, though it does not by itself say which symbols are in tension. On seventeen further phrases written to evoke two symbols at once, both intended symbols reached the top two in only $18\%$ of cases, and the margin did not set these phrases apart from single-target ones. Which readings are in play is thus for the reader to judge from the full response profile (Figure~\ref{fig:ambiguity}C2), and the margin is best used as a navigation cue. Where it is high, a system can commit to one reading, and where it is low, it can keep several readings open rather than force a single answer.
+
